[{"issue":"Hardening: `send` fails open on any dialog it does not recognise. `assertSendable` refuses only when the screen matches a fixed list of trust and permission wordings or a hook event is pending; otherwise `sendTurn` types the text and presses Enter up to four times (lines 565-570). It does not refuse a worker that has no session yet or is in `no_start`, which is exactly the state an unrecognised startup dialog produces. Enter on such a dialog accepts its default choice, which for the harness trust dialogs is the trusting one, so the folder's project-level config (hooks, settings) is loaded without the operator deciding. That contradicts the README's \"It never answers dialogs\" and \"Trust remains the user's decision\". Harmed principal: the operator. Attacker: the author of an untrusted folder the worker was opened in. Unverified: I did not find a current harness dialog that misses the patterns; the exposure is a harness update changing wording, or a startup dialog outside the list (unverified example: Claude's project MCP-server approval when `--mcp` is passed).","file":"lib/agent.js","line":"537","quote":"  if (w.approvalPending || result.state === 'stuck' || dialog) {","suggestion":"Make `send` refuse when no turn could accept the text: exit non-zero when the worker has neither a session nor a transcript, or when refresh returns `no_start`, with a message that names the state. Follow-ups only make sense after a session exists, so this costs nothing in normal use.","likelihood":2,"impact":5,"protected":false,"trigger":"`agent wait <id>` returns `no_start` (exit 6) because a startup dialog the patterns do not match is on screen, and the orchestrator then runs `agent send <id> \"...\"` to nudge the worker. No test or documented flow sends before a session; it is reachable but uncommon.","preExisting":false,"risk":10,"severity":"low","filed":true,"status":"verified-quote","source":"security","id":"s2","review":"2026-11-16-20-30-44-7c058b","finding":"s2","decision":"filed"},{"issue":"Regression from the s1 fix: a Codex worker opened in a subdirectory of a linked worktree gets no Git grant at all and cannot commit. The backlink check compares the administration directory's `gitdir` file with `$worktree/.git`, where `worktree` is `$(pwd -P)`, the launch cwd. Git records the worktree root's `.git` there, so from `<worktree>/packages/foo` the comparison fails and neither the data directories nor the administration directory is added. Before the fix the common directory was granted whenever it lay outside the cwd, so this case could commit. A subdirectory of a normal (non-linked) checkout also lost its grant, but that one cannot be narrowed because its Git directory is the common root.","file":"lib/agent.js","line":"470","quote":"      '        if [ \"$backlink\" = \"$worktree/.git\" ]; then',","suggestion":"Compare the backlink with the physical path of `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` plus `/.git` instead of the cwd. The forged-link check still holds, because a crafted folder's toplevel is the crafted folder and the victim's backlink does not point to it.","likelihood":2,"impact":5,"protected":false,"trigger":"`agent open sol --cwd <linked worktree>/<subdir> ...`, for example a package folder in a monorepo worktree; the worker's `git commit` then fails on `index.lock` under the sandbox. The standard builder path opens at the worktree root and is not affected.","preExisting":false,"risk":10,"severity":"low","filed":true,"status":"verified-quote","id":"x3","inFix":true,"review":"fix-1794862432890-32daf4","finding":"x3","decision":"filed"},{"issue":"The f2 refusal has no exit when there is no leftover input to submit. `pendingSend` is saved at line 590 before the text is typed at line 591, and the new check refuses every later send while it is unconfirmed, without checking that the text is still in the composer. If `cmux send` throws (it has a 10 s timeout), or the operator clears the stale text in the pane instead of submitting it, the worker refuses all sends with exit 5 and `wait` reports `unsent` indefinitely. The message tells the operator to submit input that is not there; the only ways out are typing some other turn in the pane or closing the worker. Before the fix the next send re-armed `pendingSend`.","file":"lib/agent.js","line":"571","quote":"  if (w.pendingSend && !w.pendingSend.confirmedAt) {","suggestion":"Keep the sent text in `pendingSend` and refuse only while it is still visible on the worker's screen; when it is gone and no new turn started, drop the stale attempt and let the new send proceed. Also set `pendingSend` only after `cmux send` returns.","likelihood":2,"impact":4,"protected":false,"trigger":"`agent send` fails as unsent, the operator deletes the leftover text in the worker pane rather than pressing Enter, and the orchestrator sends the replacement instructions; or `cmux send` errors after the record is saved.","preExisting":false,"risk":8,"severity":"low","filed":true,"status":"verified-quote","id":"x4","inFix":true,"review":"fix-1794862432890-32daf4","finding":"x4","decision":"filed"},{"issue":"Incomplete repair of f4: the re-export pattern still misses one compact form. After `\\*`, the optional alias group `(?:\\s+as\\s+\\w+)?` requires whitespace before `as`, so `export*as worker from './agent.js';` (valid module syntax) matches neither alternative, and the dynamic import/require scan does not see it either. The specifier is never collected, so lib/agent.js can be reachable from server.js while guard b passes. The fix made `export{...}from` and `export*from` match and pinned only the `export{openWorker}from` form in setup-guards.mjs. Evidence: static reading of the regex at HEAD against that input; not executed (no shell in this session). Same family, also unexecuted: the import pattern's `(?:^|[;\\n])` anchor on line 1063 misses an import that directly follows a block's closing brace, e.g. `function f(){}import'./lib/agent.js';`.","file":"tests/guards.mjs","line":"1064","quote":"      /\\bexport\\s*(?:\\*(?:\\s+as\\s+\\w+)?|\\{[^}]*\\})\\s*from\\s*(['\"])([^'\"]+)\\1/g,","suggestion":"Let the namespace alternative tolerate any alias text, mirroring the import pattern: `/\\bexport\\s*(?:\\*[^'\";]*?|\\{[^}]*\\})\\s*from\\s*(['\"])([^'\"]+)\\1/g`. Add a pin in tests/setup-guards.mjs: `export*as worker from './agent.js';` on registry.js with checkSyntax true.","likelihood":1,"impact":7,"protected":false,"trigger":"No path in normal use: a module in the server's import graph would have to re-export the worker with the whitespace-free namespace form `export*as name from './agent.js'`, which neither hand-written code nor a formatter in this repo produces.","preExisting":false,"risk":7,"severity":"low","filed":true,"status":"verified-quote","id":"x1","inFix":true,"review":"fix-1794866876748-a2a437","finding":"x1","decision":"filed"}]

Your shell is sandboxed read-only and cannot use bash process substitution — `<(...)` fails with "Operation not permitted". Use node one-liners or write intermediate output to files instead.

Every finding MUST carry `quote`: one to three lines copied character-for-character from `file` as it is at HEAD, starting at or within 5 lines of `line` (a single line number). Open the file and copy it — never paraphrase or reconstruct it from memory. A script checks every quote against the file; a finding whose quote is not there is discarded as unlocatable.

If the same defect pattern appears in several places, report it once and list every place in `instances` (file, line and quote each, quoted exactly like the main location, which is one of them); otherwise instances is null.

`preExisting`: true when the defect exists at the base and this branch neither introduced it nor made it newly reachable; otherwise false. A script checks the claim against the diff: a finding near a line the branch changed counts as the branch's.

Score every finding; the script derives its severity from your scores.
trigger: the concrete input, command or call path in this project's normal use that reaches the defect. If none exists, say so and give likelihood 1. No real trigger means likelihood 1.
likelihood (1–10): how often this project's normal use reaches it, counting how often its prerequisites occur.
- 1: no path in normal use reaches it (no real trigger).
- 3: a named, uncommon but supported path, a few in 100 uses.
- 5: a regular optional path, about 1 in 10 uses.
- 7: most uses of that feature.
- 10: every use, deterministic.
impact (1–10):
- 1: cosmetic.
- 3: friction (minutes lost, a misleading message), with the result still correct.
- 5: one visible failed or wrong run, redone.
- 7: silently wrong, a safeguard doesn't apply, or the main workflow is blocked.
- 10: can't be undone (data lost without ordinary undo, an exploitable hole, wrong money).
A failure nobody would notice scores one band higher.
protected: true only when you can demonstrate one of these four cases:
- a reachable, exploitable security hole;
- stored data destroyed or overwritten beyond ordinary undo (if revert, reflog, trash or a re-run restores it, it is not protected);
- wrong money;
- a way around the review gate in the gate's own logic: the push check, the close, a dispute or a
  waiver accepting work it should refuse. A command phrasing the agent guard (agent-guard) doesn't
  recognize is not protected: score it by likelihood × impact like any other bug, because the guard
  is an accident barrier.
Protected findings are HIGH however rare, and never filed.
risk = likelihood × impact:
- HIGH when protected, or risk >= 40;
- LOW when risk <= 10 and not protected; LOW is filed, not fixed unless the operator picks it;
- MEDIUM otherwise.
Fix HIGH first, then MEDIUM. Both block until fixed, REFUTED or waived by the operator.
Missing or invalid scores are asked for again once. A finding still unscored blocks as MEDIUM (HIGH if protected is exactly true).

